A||||5||IwannaIRM||Iron Duke Clarification||||5||
Z||000000||IwannaIRM||08-07-2001||04:57 PM||||Ok, correct if I am wrong...which happens more times than not.<P>I thought the Iron Duke was only available in 1984.  Then after all of the oil leaks, engine fires, and breakdowns Pontiac switched over to the Tech 4.<P>And out of the 84's there were only so many "True" Iron Dukes.  These were identified by special casting numbers on the block.<P>Where my confusion stems is I see people with 87's and 88's calling their motors Iron Dukes when they aren't (I don't think they are).<P>Am I wrong?  Please straighten me out.<P>Steve||||reg||5
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||08-07-2001||05:26 PM||||They are Iron Dukes, The 4tech was just a name given to the Injection system. The blocks were the same except for 87-88 having a new distributor_less ignition system. It was still the "Iron Duke" 2.5 engine. It still used the same pistons,heads,valves,cam. It is a true duke.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000002||Oreif||08-07-2001||06:40 PM||||Thge 2.5L "Iron Duke" came out in 1977. The term Iron Duke was to advertise it wasn't the aluminum 4's used previously in the Vega/Astre cars. The Iron duke was in Sunbirds/Monzas/Skyhawks. In 1984 the 2.5L was then throttle body injected with a swirl-port head design. The upgraded head/induction was called Tech4.<BR>So all GM 2.5L's were "Dukes"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Roscoe P. Coltrane||08-07-2001||06:53 PM||||Never you mind about them Dukes.  They ain't no good no how.  But me an' Boss, we got 'em right where we want 'em this time.  Kee kee kee!||||reg||12
Z||000004||IwannaIRM||08-07-2001||07:24 PM||||Thanks for the clarification.<P>Where's Daisy?||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||08-07-2001||07:54 PM||||They are all called iron duke. To most people they are all look the same. It's not untill you get into details you find out how diferent some versions are.<P>Actually 87 and up are only partly related to the original and TBI distributor motors. The head, block, crank, intake, and TBI are all diferant and incompatible with the older distributor motor. Most 88 and up car version motors also got a balance unit to reduce vibration. There is also a version of the DIS motor for S10 trucks.<P>Electrically they share almost nothing. A couple non ECM sensors are about it.<P>About the only thing truely common between the DIS and older motors are maybe a few internal parts and they physically fit the car and tranny.||||reg||1
